# Dead-branch domain safety. The else-arm divides by zero, but the condition `a == a`
# is always true, so that arm is DEAD at runtime. The prover's circuit model builds
# BOTH arms of an `if`, though — so Glass cannot faithfully lower this program. The
# honest verdict is ABSTAIN (refuse), NOT a REJECT (which would wrongly read as "the
# statement is false") and NOT a silently-wrong proof.
#
#   glass prove examples/prove/deadbranch_abstain.glass a=7 b=0    # -> verdict: ABSTAIN
#
# (Added v5.86: the all-branches domain guard catches the out-of-domain op in the dead
#  arm and abstains. See docs/roadmap.md "The convergence" for the deeper fix — predicated
#  constraints — that would let the dead arm impose nothing and prove the live result.)

if a == a then a else a % b
